        Chapter 1: Introduction                                         1-1









                             CHAPTER 1 - Introduction



        Under AOS/VS, via the ?WHIST system call,  a  user  may  perform  a
        histogram against either a single process or against all processes.
        AOS/VS will return raw histogram data in a pre-determined format.

        Histograms record the number of clock periods or "ticks"  spent  in
        specific logical address ranges or  "buckets"  over  the  histogram
        duration.  At the end of each tick of the clock,  AOS/VS  evaluates
        the value of  the  program  counter  and  determines  which  bucket
        counter is to be incremented.   While  histograms  do  not  provide
        exact information  and  though  a  statistical  deviation  must  be
        anticipated, histograms  do  provide  an  indication  of  how  much
        process run time is  being  consumed  within  the  specific  bucket
        ranges.

        The system calls ?WHIST and ?KHIST permit the user to  histogram  a
        user-specified logical address range.  With this  information,  the
        user has the means to identify and rectify inefficient sections  of
        his program's code.  Through repetitive histograms over  increasin-
        gly smaller intervals, measurements of the smallest  intervals  may
        be made.

        The histogram data may indicate that an excess amount  of  time  is
        being spent in a particular routine.  This would  suggest  that  an
        optimization of this routine would  significantly  improve  program
        performance.

        The histogram utilities, HISTO and HISTOREPORT, facilitate the  use
        of this histogram functionality.  HISTO  will  perform  the  ?WHIST
        system call in accordance with user specifications, and generate  a
        datafile containing the raw histogram data as  well  as  additional
        pertinent information.  HISTOREPORT will analyze this  datafile  in
        accordance with user specifications, and will produce a report file
        containing the interpreted histogram data.

             HISTO/HISTOREPORT support includes the following:

              * 16-bit and 32-bit target processes are supported.

              * Full ring support.  Addresses in rings 0 through 7  may  be
                monitored, and symbol table files may be provided  for  any
                ring.




        Data General Corporation                       Company Confidential
        
        HISTO/HISTOREPORT - Histogramming Utilities, Rev. 1          Page 2
        Chapter 1: Introduction                                         1-2



           * Clock tick totals for various  segments  of  the  system,  the
                target process, and other processes.

              * A report detailing the limits of each bucket, the number of
                ticks recorded in that bucket,  and  the  number  of  ticks
                expressed as a  percentage  of  the  total  ticks  recorded
                within the whole histogram range.


             HISTO/HISTOREPORT optionally provide the following:

              * Symbolic representations for the starting logical addresses
                of each bucket.



                                 [End of Chapter 1]






































