        Chapter 2: The Data Collection Utility (HISTO)                  2-1









                  CHAPTER 2 - The Data Collection Utility (HISTO)



        This program histograms  the  target  process(es)  via  the  ?WHIST
        supervisor call and, upon completion of the histogram,  writes  the
        raw histogram data out to a datafile in a form intelligible to  the
        report program (HISTOREPORT.PR).

        The user specifies the parameters defining the target  process(es),
        the duration of the histogram, the logical starting address of  the
        area to be histogramed, the respective logical ending address,  the
        size of each bucket within this range, and the file to receive  the
        raw data.

        The utility process requires at execution the privilege  to  become
        resident; this is the only restricted privilege required.


        2.1  Operating Procedures


        Note:   Before  HISTO  can  be  invoked,  the  target  must
                already have been initialized,  as  HISTO  requires
                the target pid number as an initial argument.

        HISTO is invoked by the following command line:

                XEQ HISTO <pid#> <run> <saddr> <eaddr> <bucket> <datafile>

        User parameters to HISTO are specified as arguments on the  command
        line.  There are six required arguments:

        <pid#>
                        The pid of the  target  process,  or  zero  if  the
                        target is "all processes".

        <run>
                        Maximum  histogram  duration, in  seconds.     HISTO
                        will not  terminate  until  the specified  number of
                        seconds  has  elapsed, even if  the  target  process
                        terminates.







        Data General Corporation                       Company Confidential
        HISTO/HISTOREPORT - Histogramming Utilities, Rev. 1          Page 4
        Chapter 2: The Data Collection Utility (HISTO)                  2-2



        <saddr>
                        The starting address of the area to be histogramed.
                        This value must be  specified  in  octal  and  must
                        include a ring field; a ring field of zero  indica-
                        tes a ring zero address.  This value corresponds to
                        offset ?HWST in the ?WHIST packet.

        <eaddr>
                        The ending address of the area to  be  histogramed.
                        This value must be  specified  in  octal  and  must
                        include a ring field; a ring field of zero  indica-
                        tes a ring zero address.  This value corresponds to
                        offset ?HWEND in the ?WHIST packet.

        <bucket>
                        The size of each interval in  the  histogram  area.
                        This value must be specified in octal.  This  value
                        corresponds to offset ?HWWDS in the ?WHIST packet.

        <datafile>
                        Pathname of the file to receive the histogram data.
                        This file must not already exist.



                                 [End of Chapter 2]




























